# <type>[optional scope]: <imperative description>
#
# Why is this change needed?
#
# What evidence proves it works?
#
# Optional footers:
# BREAKING CHANGE: <consumer impact and migration path>
# Refs: <issue, ADR, or RFC>
#
# Subject rules:
#   - use imperative mood: "add", not "added" or "adds"
#   - keep it under 50 characters where practical
#   - do not end it with a period
#   - keep one logical change per commit
#
# Types:
#   feat:     consumer-visible capability
#   fix:      user-facing bug or vulnerability
#   perf:     behavior-preserving performance improvement
#   refactor: restructuring without external behavior change
#   docs:     documentation only
#   style:    formatting or another non-semantic change
#   test:     tests only
#   build:    build system or bundling
#   ci:       CI/CD or workflow automation
#   chore:    dependencies, tooling, or maintenance
#   revert:   revert a prior commit
#
# Common scopes:
#   core, kernel, http, auth, db, cli, compiler, testing,
#   template, plugin, mcp, docs, modules, libraries, deps
#
# Breaking changes:
#   Use feat!: / fix!: or include a BREAKING CHANGE footer.
#
# Release note:
#   Conventional Commit types describe the change. Changesets—not the
#   commit prefix—control package versions and package changelogs.
